i='TCS-2665';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='ITCS 2022, article 78: Definitions 1.1–1.2 and Open Problem 1.5 read. The truncated target compares bit queries with the sample complexity in the same DoHO distance model, at ε/2.'
refs.extend([dict(id='full2023',title='Testing Distributions of Huge Objects — corrected full version',authors='Oded Goldreich, Dana Ron',year=2023,url='https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~oded/R3/doho.pdf',locator='Author-hosted full version dated 23 May 2023; journal publication in TheoretiCS 2, article 12. Open Problem 1.5, Theorems 1.6 and 1.9–1.10, and footnote 6 read. Explicitly corrects the conference claim: the general mapping-closed theorem uses standard-model sample complexity, not DoHO sample complexity.'),dict(id='grained2025',title='Settling the Complexity of Testing Grainedness of Distributions, and Application to Uniformity Testing in the Huge Object Model',authors='Clément L. Canonne, Sayantan Sen, Joy Qiping Yang',year=2025,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2025.26',locator='ITCS 2025, article 26: Theorems 1.1 and 1.3–1.4 read. Proves an Ω(m/log m) query lower bound for parameterized uniformity when m≤n and a different O(m^{2/3}n) upper bound for other parameter regimes; does not classify all label-invariant properties.')])
issue='The source asks for a characterization of the properties satisfying the comparison, rather than asserting that every label-invariant property does. It does not specify an admissible form or effective representation for the desired characterization. A precise single benchmark needs that criterion and its uniformity conventions; listing more examples or restating the inequality as the definition of the class is not a characterization of the intended kind.'
save(i,dict(title='Which huge-object properties need only a few bit queries per sample?',formal='Characterize the label-invariant distribution properties whose DoHO query complexity is at most poly(1/ε)·Õ(s(n,ε/2)), where s is the sample complexity of testing the same property in the DoHO model. Both complexities use earth-mover distance induced by normalized Hamming distance on n-bit strings. The source gives positive and negative examples, so the target is a classification rather than a universal bound for every property.',definitions='For each n≥1, a property Dₙ is a set of probability distributions on Ωₙ={0,1}ⁿ. It is label-invariant if P∈Dₙ exactly when π_*P∈Dₙ for every bijection π:Ωₙ→Ωₙ, where (π_*P)(y)=P(π^{-1}(y)). This permits arbitrary permutations of all strings, not merely permutations of bit coordinates. For x,y∈Ωₙ let h(x,y)=n^{−1}|{j:xⱼ≠yⱼ}|. Define W_H(P,Q)=min_w Σ_{x,y}w(x,y)h(x,y), where w≥0 has row sums P and column sums Q. The distance to Dₙ is inf_{Q∈Dₙ}W_H(P,Q). A DoHO tester is a randomized algorithm given n and rational ε∈(0,1), with access to independent samples X¹,X²,…∼P. A sample is an oracle for an n-bit string: querying a pair (i,j) reveals only Xⁱⱼ, and further queries to that sample reveal bits of the same string. The algorithm may adapt which sample and coordinate to query. It must accept P∈Dₙ with probability at least 2/3 and reject when inf_{Q∈Dₙ}W_H(P,Q)>ε with probability at least 2/3, with no requirement in the intermediate region. Probability includes samples and internal randomness. Sample complexity counts the maximum number of independent sample oracles required; query complexity counts the maximum total number of inspected bits. The reference sample complexity permits arbitrarily many bit queries to those samples. The comparison seeks only polynomial losses in 1/ε and logarithmic factors in the sample bound, not a factor n for reading every sampled object. It does not compare against total-variation testing in the standard model, where each sample is revealed in full and closeness has a different definition.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='Specify and prove a non-tautological necessary-and-sufficient characterization for the label-invariant property families satisfying the stated query-versus-DoHO-sample comparison, with explicit uniformity and polynomial/logarithmic losses. The source does not yet specify the form of this characterization. A sufficient condition alone, a result comparing to standard-model sample complexity, or bounds for a single property do not complete the general classification.',context_blocks=[b('A sample can be much larger than the tester can read','The input distribution generates n-bit objects. Access to one sampled object does not reveal its entire label for free; the tester pays separately for each inspected bit.', 'primary'),b('Distance accounts for the geometry of the objects','Moving probability mass between similar strings costs their relative Hamming distance. This earth-mover metric can be much smaller than total variation, even for a label-invariant property.', 'primary'),b('The conference version overclaimed one general transfer','The corrected full version explicitly retracts its earlier use of DoHO sample complexity in the theorem for properties closed under arbitrary mappings. The corrected theorem starts from standard-model sample complexity, a weaker comparison. It leaves the stronger DoHO-sample version open.', 'full2023'),b('There are both positive and negative cases','Bounded-support testing gives a positive example. For uniformity over a prescribed number m of strings, bit-query complexity can be nearly linear in m even though only about m^{2/3} full samples are needed at constant accuracy. Thus label invariance by itself is insufficient.', 'full2023'),b('The 2025 refinement remains a special-property result','For parameterized uniformity and m≤n, the new theorem gives an Ω(m/log m) query lower bound and matches known upper bounds up to logarithms. For other ranges it supplies an O(m^{2/3}n) query algorithm, emphasizing that the parameters matter.', 'grained2025'),b('Remaining formulation issue',issue)],why='Would identify when testing a distribution of enormous objects can exploit sampling without paying to read each object, clarifying the relationship between statistical information and local access cost.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2022',text='Open Problem 1.5 asks which label-invariant properties permit bit-query complexity close to their DoHO sample complexity.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2023-05-23',text='The full version corrects the general mapping-closed theorem to use standard-model sample complexity, and retains the stronger question.',citation='full2023'),dict(date='2025',text='ITCS 2025 settles the parameterized-uniformity query complexity up to logarithmic factors for m≤n, without classifying all properties.',citation='grained2025')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the primary model and full question, the corrected May 2023 version including its explicit erratum, and the 2025 uniformity/grainedness results. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no general classification. The form of the requested characterization still needs specification.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',remaining_issue=issue,notes=['Recovered the full complexity comparison and corrected the conference theorem against the later version.']),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A general relationship between sample information and local bit access when observations are large structured objects. Existing numerical score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Restored the missing Õ(s(n,ε/2)) factor and the exact earth-mover access model. Recorded the later correction to Theorem 1.6 rather than propagating it, distinguished label and coordinate invariance, and left the classification criterion unresolved.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The DoHO model samples long bit strings but charges the tester for every inspected bit.','Distance between distributions is earth-mover distance using normalized Hamming costs.','The source asks which label-invariant properties need only polynomial-in-accuracy and logarithmic overhead over their DoHO sample complexity.','Its corrected full version weakens one claimed general transfer theorem, while a 2025 paper sharpens bounds for a particular uniformity property.','A precise general characterization criterion remains to be specified.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full2023','grained2025'])),notes=['Restored the query bound in terms of DoHO sample complexity at ε/2.','Defined adaptive sample-oracle access, normalized Hamming earth-mover distance and label invariance.','Applied the explicit correction to the conference mapping-closed theorem.','Distinguished the later uniformity special case from a full property classification.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~/oded/p_doho.html'],state='improved_unfinished')
